-120875" :question/problem "Researchers surveyed students in School A on which superpower they would most like to have. The results of this survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Flying | Invisibility | Other | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female | $16$ | $18$ | $12$ | $46$ |\n| Male | $28$ | $12$ | $14$ | $54$ |\n| Totals | $44$ | $30$ | $26$ | $100$ |\n\nSchool B has $300$ students. Assuming the distribution of results is the same for both schools, estimate the number of School B students who are male and would choose to fly as their superpower."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234074 :question/math-academy-id "q-47306" :question/problem "A group of $5$th grade and $6$th grade students at Benjamin Franklin Elementary School are randomly selected for a survey. They are asked about their favorite places to go on summer vacations between the beach and the countryside. The results of this survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Beach | Countryside | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$th grade | $55$ | $30$ | $85$ |\n| $6$th grade | $42$ | $33$ | $75$ |\n| Totals | $97$ | $63$ | $160$ |\n\nJohn Adams Elementary School has $250$ students in $5$th grade and $6$th grade. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same in both elementary schools, estimate the number of students at John Adams Elementary School who are in $6$th grade or like to go to the beach on summer vacations (or both). Round your answer to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234075 :question/math-academy-id "q-47307" :question/problem "A group of randomly selected students from Chicago High School was asked which country they want to visit between Brazil, Spain, France, or Japan. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Brazil | Spain | France | Japan | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Girls | $12$ | $20$ | $9$ | $14$ | $55$ |\n| Boys | $14$ | $10$ | $20$ | $1$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $26$ | $30$ | $29$ | $15$ | $100$ |\n\nBoston High School has $400$ students. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same in both high schools, estimate the number of girls at Boston High School who wish to visit Brazil or France. Round your answer to the nearest integer."}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049705 :topic/math-academy-id 131 :topic/title "Compound Events in Probability From Experimental Data"}]}]}
